      "transcript": [
        {
          "role": "user",
          "text": "I'm planning a cast-in-situ garden studio. The net wall area is 420 square feet and the wall will be 10 inches thick. What material volume should I plan for, and how long should curing take?",
          "turn": 1,
          "at": "2026-08-22T12:36:43.281Z"
        },
        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "text": "For your 420 square foot garden studio with a 10-inch thick wall, the authoritative planning volume is **9.9 cubic metres** of hemp-lime material. \n\nFor the cast-in-situ method, formwork can typically be removed the next day after tamping, while the natural drying and curing process before applying your final breathable plaster generally takes about **3 to 6 weeks**, depending entirely on your local weather, temperature, and job-site humidity. Keep in mind that live delivery timelines, specific mix pricing, structural engineering designs, and local building permit approvals are unavailable and must be verified with qualified professionals and local authorities.\n\nWould you like to discuss the timber framing details required to support your cast-in-situ hemp-lime infill?",
